Portfolio 3 - concurrency demos (two real sessions)

=== PART 1 - the bug: check-then-insert at READ COMMITTED (slot capacity 5, 4 used) ===
  [A] counts 4 used of 5 -> looks free
  [B] counts 4 used of 5 -> looks free
  [A] INSERT booking (not committed yet)
  [B] INSERT booking (A's row is invisible to B)
  [A] COMMIT
  [B] COMMIT
  RESULT: 6 bookings in a slot of capacity 5  ->  OVERBOOKED (bug reproduced)

=== PART 2 - fix 1: SELECT ... FOR UPDATE on the slot row (what book_pickup() does) ===
  [A] locked the slot row
  [B] is waiting for A's lock (thread alive = True)
  [A] counts 4 used -> free
  [A] COMMIT (lock released)
  [B] got the lock, counted 5 used -> REJECTED: slot full
  RESULT: 5 bookings, capacity 5  ->  correct, no overbooking

=== PART 3 - fix 2: SERIALIZABLE isolation (one session is told to retry) ===
  [A] counts 4 used
  [B] counts 4 used
  [A] COMMIT ok
  [B] COMMIT refused, SQLSTATE 40001: could not serialize access due to read/write dependencies among transactions
  RESULT: 5 bookings, capacity 5  ->  correct (the loser must retry)

=== PART 4 - deadlock: A locks slot 1 then 2, B locks slot 2 then 1 ===
  [A] locked slot 631
  [B] locked slot 632
  [A] 40P01 deadlock detected
  [B] ok
  RESULT: PostgreSQL killed ['A'] with SQLSTATE 40P01; the other session completed.
  Fix: always lock slots in ascending slot_id order (then no cycle is possible).

Done.
